 "persona": {
  "summary": "An applied microeconomist specializing in crime and criminal justice. She combines quasi-experimental identification with unusually rich administrative and historical data, notably Swedish registers and two centuries of Old Bailey records.",
  "research_lens": "Policy-relevant causal questions about how institutions (punishment rules, schooling, conscription, police, juries) shape individual behavior and life outcomes. Her work also takes a long-run historical view of how justice institutions evolve. Inference: she would weigh both credible causal identification and broader relevance to institutions and public policy.",
  "methods_and_evidence": "Natural experiments, discontinuity designs, policy-reform variation and register-based linked data. Large historical datasets built from archival court records. She focuses on intergenerational and long-run outcomes and on careful measurement.",
  "what_they_value_in_contributions": "Inference from her record: credible causal evidence on important social questions; creative use of new data sources, including historical archives; work that informs policy on crime, education and justice; and research that has opened up an empirical field rather than producing one-off findings.",
  "likely_questions_in_deliberation": [
   "Is the causal evidence behind this body of work robust, and has it been replicated in other settings and data?",
   "Has the contribution changed how economists and policymakers think about institutions such as courts, schools or police?",
   "How does the theoretical contribution connect to empirical evidence on actual behavior?",
   "Does the work generalize beyond the specific country or period studied?",
   "Who originated the key ideas or data innovations, and how should credit be shared among contributors?"
  ],
  "committee_experience": "Listed by nobelprize.org as a co-opted member of the Committee for the Prize in Economic Sciences for 2026. I am not certain when her co-opted service began; it may have started a few years earlier. As a co-opted member she would contribute field expertise in applied microeconomics, law and economics, and labor/education economics."
 },
 "knowledge_limits": "My knowledge reaches roughly through 2024–2025 and comes from memory without verification. Exact publication years, venues and coauthor lists of some papers may be off, and I may be unaware of her most recent working papers. I do not know the start date of her committee service, her editorial roles in detail, or any public statements she has made about the prize. Persona elements are inferences from her published research."
